WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.364
Think of a football team. You could study each player's kicking alone, or watch how the whole team plays together. Psychologists argue about which gives a better explanation. Both ideas have names, and you need to know each one.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:33.065
Reductionism means explaining complex behaviour by breaking it down into smaller, simpler parts. It is like taking a clock apart to see how each gear works. Each small part is simpler than the whole clock.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:46.219
The Multi-store Model of memory is reductionist. It splits memory into three parts: the sensory register, short-term memory and long-term memory. Each store can then be studied on its own, for example how long short-term memory lasts.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:01:05.930
Holism means studying behaviour as a whole, because the whole is more than the sum of its parts. A holistic approach looks at how the parts interact. For memory, that means how emotion, culture and schemas affect what we recall, as in reconstructive memory.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:23.401
Each has strengths. Reductionism is easier to test scientifically but may oversimplify. Holism is more complete but harder to test. A question asks you to define a term, give an example, or describe the difference.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:36.932
Step one: define the term in one clear sentence. Step two: add an example from memory, or for a difference question, use the word whereas and define the other term.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:52.569
Each step is worth one mark. Examiners look for clear, full sentences. The trap is defining holism as just looking at everything. You must say the parts interact, and you must give an example if asked.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:05.626
Here is a real exam question. Define the term reductionism, as used in psychology, and use an example. Two marks.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:12.668
Step one: define it. Explaining a complex behaviour by breaking it down into smaller, simpler parts.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:18.890
Step two: the example. The Multi-store Model explains memory by dividing it into three separate stores.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:30.981
A tempting wrong route is to say reductionism means looking at everything together. Reject it. That is holism. Reductionism is the opposite.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:38.145
The exam answer. One sentence for the definition. One sentence for the example.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:51.362
If the question asked for holism instead, you would define the whole-behaviour idea. Then use reconstructive memory as the example, because schemas, emotion and culture all affect what we recall.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:03:00.039
One mark for the definition. One mark for the Multi-store example. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:18.855
A common mistake is to define holism as looking at everything. That is too vague. Holism means studying the whole, and how the parts interact. For memory, emotion, culture and schemas all affect each other.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:30.566
Now you try. Describe the difference between reductionism and holism. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:46.108
Define one term, say whereas, then define the other. Reductionism explains behaviour by breaking it into smaller, simpler parts, whereas holism studies the whole behaviour, because the parts interact.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:56.031
The most common wrong answer covers only one term, or says holism is looking at everything. A difference question needs both terms, compared.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:09.685
So, define the term in one clear sentence. Add an example from memory, or the other term. Use the word whereas for a difference. Write full sentences, not just a few words.
